            "target_entity_instance": "coupled",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "We use a fully coupled climate and carbon cycle model, the INtegrated Climate and Carbon model (INCCA), the NCAR/DOE Parallel Climate Model coupled to the IBIS terrestrial biosphere model and a modified OCMIP ocean biogeochemistry model.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "INCCA",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "carbon cycle feedback factor",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The 'carbon cycle feedback factor' is defined as the ratio of CO2 change when climate is changing to the CO2 change when climate is constant (Friedlingstein et al., 2003). The implied net carbon cycle feedback factor in our simulations is 1.13.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "INCCA",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_METRIC",
            "target_entity_instance": "climate sensitivity",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The climate model used by Thompson et al. (2004) has a climate sensitivity (~2 K for a doubling of CO2) near the low end of the conventionally accepted range (1.5 to 4.5 K per CO2 doubling; IPCC, 2001).",
